Host ports are the other shared resource on a machine running many tests. By default the test yaml drops the host side of every published port, the daemon assigns a free one at startup, and the runner hands the result to the test through the environment: Variable Value RULES_DOCKER_COMPOSE_HOST_<SERVICE>_<PORT> host:port the container port is reachable at, e.g. 127.0.0.1:49153 RULES_DOCKER_COMPOSE_PORT_<SERVICE>_<PORT> the host port alone <SERVICE> is the compose service name upper-cased with anything but letters and digits replaced by _, and <PORT> is the container port. ${NAME} references in test_args and in the test target's env are expanded against these at test time, so a test can be told -host $${RULES_DOCKER_COMPOSE_HOST_NGINX_80} without knowing the port in advance. Two tests publishing the same declared port therefore no longer conflict, and the declared port keeps working for bazel run. Set ephemeral_ports = False to opt out. Each run also uses its own compose project name, the target-derived name plus a random suffix, so concurrent runs of one target, or a run overlapping a crashed predecessor, never share containers or networks. Network and volume names derived from the project follow suit; names set explicitly in the compose file, like container_name, are kept as written and remain the user's responsibility. The test requires network access and a Docker daemon reachable from the host. The Docker endpoint variables are inherited from the invoking shell. Images are declared through oci_load or image_load targets in images, and every services.*.image value must equal a tag declared on one of them. The docker_compose_yaml rule documentation describes the contract, the supported loaders and the rules_img requirements in full; passing a single docker_compose_yaml target as yamls is the simplest way to share one set of images between a yaml, a test and a binary. The test is normally an ordinary *_test target written with the language's test framework and tagged manual, so bazel test //... runs it only through this rule, with the services up. Its args and env are honored, which keeps the service address next to the test that reads it. Example: load("@rules_docker_compose//docker_compose:docker_compose_test.bzl", "docker_compose_test")
load("@rules_oci//oci:defs.bzl", "oci_load")
load("@rules_python//python:defs.bzl", "py_test") oci_load( name = "my_service.load", image = ":my_service", repo_tags = ["my-service:latest"],
) py_test( name = "my_service_test", srcs = ["my_service_test.py"], env = {"SERVICE_HOST": "localhost:8080"}, # Only meaningful with the services running, so only run via :integration_test. tags = ["manual"],
) docker_compose_test( name = "integration_test", yamls = ["docker-compose.yaml"], images = [":my_service.load"], test = ":my_service_test", delay = 2, # Wait 2 seconds after containers start
) ATTRIBUTES Name Description Type Mandatory Default name A unique name for this target. Name required data Additional runtime dependencies for the test. List of labels optional [] allow_registry_images Permit digest-pinned image references that have no loader when merging raw yamls. See the docker_compose_yaml rule for details. Setting this is an error when yamls is a single docker_compose_yaml target. Boolean optional False delay Seconds to wait after containers are running before executing the test. Useful for services that need initialization time beyond their health checks. Integer optional 1 env Dictionary of strings; values are subject to $(location) and "Make variable" substitution Dictionary: String -> String optional {} env_inherit Specifies additional environment variables to inherit from the external environment when the test is executed by bazel test. The Docker endpoint variables (DOCKER_HOST, DOCKER_CONTEXT, DOCKER_CONFIG, DOCKER_CERT_PATH, DOCKER_TLS_VERIFY, DOCKER_API_VERSION) are always inherited. List of strings optional [] ephemeral_ports Let the daemon choose host ports when merging raw yamls. See the docker_compose_yaml rule for details. Setting this is an error when yamls is a single docker_compose_yaml target. Boolean optional True images Loader targets (oci_load from rules_oci or image_load from rules_img) providing the container images the compose configuration references. Every services.*.image value must equal a tag declared on one of these targets. See the rule documentation for details. List of labels optional [] interpolate Whether to resolve ${VAR} references when merging raw yamls at build time. See the docker_compose_yaml rule for details.Setting this is an error when yamls is a single docker_compose_yaml target, which carries its own interpolate setting.Note that bazel test runs with a sanitized environment, so a deferred variable also needs to be listed in env_inherit (or set in env) to reach docker-compose at runtime. Boolean optional True interpolation_env Environment variables used to interpolate raw yamls at build time. See the docker_compose_yaml rule for details.This is distinct from env, which sets the environment of the test process itself. Setting this is an error when yamls is a single docker_compose_yaml target; declare it on that target instead. Dictionary: String -> String optional {} startup_timeout Seconds to wait for every service to be running, and healthy when it declares a healthcheck, before the test is considered failed. Integer optional 60 test The executable to run once the services are up. This is usually a *_test target (py_test, rust_test, go_test, ...) tagged manual so it only runs here; its args and env are honored. A plain binary works too. Arguments from test_args take precedence. Label optional None test_args Arguments to pass to the test binary. ${NAME} references are expanded at test time against the test's environment, including the port variables; write them as $${NAME} in BUILD files. List of strings optional [] yamls One or more docker-compose YAML files defining the services to run. Files are merged using docker-compose config. List of labels optional []

